tel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Implementation team is contractor-dependent with contract expiry risk and potential 6-month restart delay","lead":"A \u00a34.6m capital investment critically dependent on contractor continuity faces execution risk. Loss of momentum could force a 6-month restart, adding cost or potentially stranding the prior \u00a34.2m investment if the project cannot recover. This is a key\u2026","iso":"2026-06-25","paper_title":"Housing Management System (HMS)_ Additional Funding Request","paper_url":"https://kingston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s114360/114360.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Capital project has grown 76% beyond initial budget, now requiring additional HRA borrowing","lead":"A 76% cost overrun on a capital project, funded through incremental HRA borrowing approvals (November 2023, September 2025, delegated authority, and now June 2026), signals either poor initial scoping or significant scope creep. For a lender assessing the\u2026","iso":"2026-06-25","paper_title":"Housing Management System (HMS)_ Additional Funding Request","paper_url":"https://kingston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s114360/114360.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-06-25","committee":"Corporate and Resources Committee","paper_title":"Housing Management System (HMS)_ Additional Funding Request","paper_url":"https://kingston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s114360/114360.pdf","headline":"Phase 3 promised 'substantial decrease in contact centre demand' and 'cashable efficiencies' are unquantified and unproven","detail":"Phase 3 is presented as delivering 'a substantial decrease in contact centre demand' and 'the realisation of cashable efficiencies across the council'. However, the paper does not quantify the scale or timing of these savings. Phases 1 and 2 only recently went live (Phase 1 December 2025, Phase 2 still rolling out Spring/Summer 2026), so there is no evidence yet that completed phases are delivering measurable operational gains or cost reductions.","category":"savings","matters":"The council is committing additional HRA borrowing partly on the basis of future operational efficiencies that are neither quantified nor yet validated by completed phases. If Phase 3 savings do not materialise, the council carries debt burden without offsetting benefits. This is a key risk for assessing financial sustainability.","figures":"Phase 3 promises 'substantial decrease in contact centre demand' and 'cashable efficiencies'\u2014amounts and timescales not specified","quote":"Phase 3 (Engage Modules) will introduce digitally enabled, resident self-service giving tenants the capacity to review, amend and update their housing account. As well as being a vast improvement for tenants, this change is expected to result in a substantial decrease in contact centre demand and an enhancement in data accuracy.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-06-25","committee":"Corporate and Resources Committee","paper_title":"Housing Management System (HMS)_ Additional Funding Request","paper_url":"https://kingston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s114360/114360.pdf","headline":"Implementation team is contractor-dependent with contract expiry risk and potential 6-month restart delay","detail":"The Digital Implementation Team critical to delivery is 'largely composed of contractors'. If the team departs before project completion, 'skills and knowledge will be lost' and 'a restart of the project may require up to 6 months to onboard new resources'. The NEC contract expires 31 July 2026, while Phases 3 and 4 are targeted for October 2026\u2014only a 3-month completion window after contract end. This creates execution risk and potential for stranded investment.","category":"governance","matters":"A \u00a34.6m capital investment critically dependent on contractor continuity faces execution risk. Loss of momentum could force a 6-month restart, adding cost or potentially stranding the prior \u00a34.2m investment if the project cannot recover. This is a key dependency for lenders assessing project viability.","figures":"Contract expires 31 July 2026; Phases 3&4 due October 2026 (3-month window); 6-month potential restart delay if paused","quote":"There is a risk that should the Implementation Team depart prematurely before all planned modules can fully be implemented, skills and knowledge will be lost as the team is largely composed of contractors. A pause in the project implementation will mean contractors will be released and a restart of the project may require up to 6 months to onboard new resources to restart the project.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-06-25","committee":"Corporate and Resources Committee","paper_title":"Housing Management System (HMS)_ Additional Funding Request","paper_url":"https://kingston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s114360/114360.pdf","headline":"Capital project has grown 76% beyond initial budget, now requiring additional HRA borrowing","detail":"The NEC Housing Management System project was budgeted at \u00a32.615m when approved in November 2023. Following scope expansion for data cleansing and system integration, combined with implementation delays, the council approved \u00a34.220m. This request adds a further \u00a3372,088, bringing the total to \u00a34.592m. The council attributes the growth to 'additional work required to cleanse and rationalise its data' and notes 'the programme has also incurred delays as a result of the completion of this additional work'.","category":"capital","matters":"A 76% cost overrun on a capital project, funded through incremental HRA borrowing approvals (November 2023, September 2025, delegated authority, and now June 2026), signals either poor initial scoping or significant scope creep. For a lender assessing the council's financial position, this adds to debt burden without yet-proven benefits from earlier phases.","figures":"Initial budget (Nov 2023): \u00a32.615m; Current approved: \u00a34.220m; Additional requested: \u00a3372,088; Total projected: \u00a34.592m (76% growth)","quote":"Accordingly the Council has revisited the budget for the NEC implementation programme with an additional \u00a31.160m approved by the Corporate & Resources committee in September 2025. A further budget of \u00a3445k was subsequently approved under delegated authority bringing the total current budget for the NEC Housing Management System project at \u00a34,219,761.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-06-25","committee":"Corporate and Resources Committee","paper_title":"Soft FM Contract Award","paper_url":"https://kingston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s114365/114365.pdf","headline":"Soft FM costs consistently exceed the dedicated annual budget allocation of \u00a31.10m and are absorbed through reallocation within the wider Property and Projects budget.","detail":"The council acknowledges that anticipated service costs are higher than the annual FM budget of \u00a31.10m, creating a budget pressure. However, the council notes that these costs have historically been contained within the wider Property and Projects Service budgets through betterment in areas such as commercial rental income. The new contract value is \u00a37.51m excluding VAT over five years, broadly in line with historic spend. The council assumes this ongoing cost can continue to be managed within existing resources.","category":"overspend","matters":"A lender should understand that the council is managing a structural gap between dedicated FM budgets and actual costs through cross-service reallocation, which depends on achieving betterment in other service areas like commercial property income.","figures":"Dedicated FM budget: \u00a31.10m annually; New contract value: \u00a37.51m excluding VAT over five years; Contract term: 3 years with optional 2-year extension","quote":"It is noted that the anticipated cost of the services is higher than the annual budget of \u00a31.10m which presents a pressure to the Council's FM budget. However, the total cost of the new contract is broadly in line with the historic cost of the services which has been contained within the wider Property and Projects Service budgets.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-06-25","committee":"Corporate and Resources Committee","paper_title":"Revenue and Capital Outturn Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://kingston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s114198/114198.pdf","headline":"Placement costs for children in care rising 8.4% above inflation, driven by complexity and market shortages","detail":"Children Looked After services overspent by \u00a31.03m. The shift in case mix is material: fewer children in lower-cost in-house foster care (39 budgeted vs 35 actual) and more in expensive independent fostering agencies (28 budgeted vs 34 actual) and children's homes, due to placement complexity. Average placement costs rose 8.4% against an inflationary rise included in the budget. Children's cases also show increased complexity requiring higher-cost support. This is part of a wider pattern: ASC operations overspent \u00a30.83m partly due to complexity and market pressures in adult care.","category":"demand","matters":"Rising placement costs outpacing inflation and budget assumptions is a leading indicator of social care market stress. It suggests either the council underestimated complexity or the care market is becoming more expensive. Both are concerning for councils' medium-term spending.","figures":"Children Looked After overspend \u00a31.03m; placement cost inflation 8.4% vs budgeted inflation; 34 independent fostering placements vs 28 budgeted; 25 residential placements; ASC Operations overspend \u00a30.83m","quote":"The average cost of placements has also exceeded the inflationary rise included in the budget for all placement types - seeing an increase on average of 8.40%.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-06-25"},"name":"Kingston Upon Thames","findings_total":92,"news_drafts_total":3}}